==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰerǵʰ-|*dʰeh₁-}}
From {{bor|en|fro|fortifier}}, from {{der|en|la|fortificō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈfɔɹtɪfaɪ/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈfɔːtɪfaɪ/}}
* {{hyph|en|for|ti|fy}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-fortify.wav|Audio (UK)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|military}} To increase the [[defense]]s of; to [[strengthen]] and [[secure]] by military works; to render defensible against an attack by [[hostile]] forces. {{defdate|from early 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[impart]] [[strength]] or [[vigor]] to.
#* {{RQ:Scott Guy|passage=Pride came to the aid of fancy, and both combined to '''fortify''' his resolution.}}
#* {{RQ:Wodehouse Offing|chapter=XXI|passage=“And do you realize that in a few shakes I've got to show up at dinner and have Mrs Cream being very, very kind to me? It hurts the pride of the Woosters, Jeeves.” “My advice, sir, would be to '''fortify''' yourself for the ordeal.” “How?” “There are always cocktails, sir. Should I pour you another?” “You should.”}}
# {{lb|en|wine}} To add [[spirit]]s to [[wine]] to increase the [[alcohol]] content. {{defdate|from 1880}}
#: {{ux|en|Sherry is made by '''fortifying''' wine.}}
# {{lb|en|food}} To increase the [[nutritional]] value of food by adding [[ingredient]]s. {{defdate|from 1939}}
#* '''1979''', ''Kiplinger's Personal Finance'' (volume 33, number 7, July 1979, page 47)
#*: Compare the nutrition information label of a regular ready-to-eat '''fortified''' cereal with that of a presweetened brand and you'll note that, although the sweetened one's sugar content is higher, the fortification is virtually identical.
#: {{ux|en|Soy milk is often '''fortified''' with calcium.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|To strengthen military defenses}} {{l|en|castellate}}, {{l|en|incastle}}, {{l|en|incastellate}}; ''see also'' '''{{l|en|strengthen}}''' ''and'' '''{{l|en|secure}}'''
* {{sense|To impart strength}} See also [[Thesaurus:strengthen]]

